For a commutative ring $R$, polynomials $f,g\in R[x]$, and $c\in R$:

1. $(f+g)'=f'+g'$ and $(cf)'=cf'$;
2. $(x^n)'=n x^{n-1}$ for every positive $n$, while every constant has derivative $0$;
3. $(fg)'=f'g+fg'$;
4. if $f'$ is nonzero, then $\deg f'\le\deg f-1$.

If $G=\langle g\rangle$ is cyclic, then exactly one of the following applies:

- if $g$ has infinite order, $G\cong(\mathbb Z,+)$;
- if $g$ has finite order $n$, necessarily $n\ge1$, then $G\cong(\mathbb Z/n,+)$.

